\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Accretive complex Gaussian integrals and cancellation}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{V} be a finite-dimensional complex Hermitian space, of
dimension #{n}, and let #{h} be its Hermitian product,
conjugate-linear in the first argument as in the [determinant action of a fixed connection](fdip-0070).
Call #{K\in\operatorname{End}(V)} \newvocab{strictly accretive}
when #{\operatorname{Re}h(z,Kz)>0} for every #{z\ne0}.
Equivalently, the Hermitian part #{H=(K+K^{\dagger})/2} is positive
definite, because #{\operatorname{Re}h(z,Kz)=h(z,Hz)}. Let
#{\extd\operatorname{vol}} be the canonical real volume of this
Hermitian product: in any orthonormal basis,
#{z_j=x_j+iy_j} and
#{\extd\operatorname{vol}=\prod_j \extd x_j\,\extd y_j}. A unitary change of
basis has real Jacobian of absolute value one, so the volume
does not depend on that basis. Then #{K} is invertible, the
following integral converges absolutely, and
##{I(K):=\int_V \ue^{-h(z,Kz)}\,\extd\operatorname{vol}
       =\frac{\pi^n}{\det K}.}
With #{Z_B(K)=\pi^{-n}I(K)} and the
[paired Grassmann value](fdip-008H)
#{Z_F(K)=\int\extd\bar\psi\,\extd\psi\;(\ue^{-\bar\psi(K\psi)})},
##{Z_B(K)Z_F(K)=1.}
For #{n=0} both factors are one.}

\p{Accretivity survives an orthogonal Schur complement. Split
#{V=R\oplus E} by an orthogonal direct sum, and let
#{A,B,C,D,S} be the typed compressions of \ref{fdip-009J} for
the orthogonal projections, with #{E} eliminated. Then #{D} is
strictly accretive, hence invertible, and #{S} is strictly
accretive. For #{v\ne0} the graph vector
#{x=i_Rv-i_ED^{-1}Cv} satisfies #{Kx=i_RSv} and
#{h(x,Kx)=h(v,Sv)}. This uses accretivity of #{K}, not only
invertibility of #{D}.}

\proof{
\p{For #{n>0}, choose #{\alpha>0} with #{H\ge\alpha\bbI}. Then
##{|\ue^{-h(z,Kz)}|
 =\ue^{-\operatorname{Re}h(z,Kz)}
 =\ue^{-h(z,Hz)}
 \le \ue^{-\alpha\|z\|^2}.}
The right side is integrable by the product of scalar Gaussian
integrals. If #{Kz=0}, then #{h(z,Hz)=0}, so #{z=0}; finite
dimension makes #{K} invertible.}

\p{First suppose #{K=K^{\dagger}>0}. Choose an orthonormal eigenbasis,
with positive eigenvalues #{\lambda_j}. In these coordinates
the unitary change from any other orthonormal basis preserves
#{\extd\operatorname{vol}}. In each complex coordinate the
[scalar Gaussian formula](fdip-002T) gives
##{\int_{\mathbb R^2}\ue^{-\lambda_j(x^2+y^2)}\,\extd x\,\extd y
       =\frac\pi{\lambda_j}.}
Multiplication gives #{I(K)=\pi^n/\det K}.}

\p{For general #{K}, write #{K=H+A} with #{A^{\dagger}=-A}, and set
#{K(w)=H+wA} for a complex parameter #{w}. Its Hermitian part
is #{H+i(\operatorname{Im}w)A}. Choose #{\epsilon>0} such that
#{\epsilon\|A\|<\frac{\alpha}{2}}, with any positive choice if #{A=0}.
On the connected strip #{|\operatorname{Im}w|<\epsilon}, this
part is at least #{\alpha\bbI /2}, so #{K(w)} is invertible.}

\p{The integral #{I(K(w))} is holomorphic on that strip.
Differentiation of the integrand brings down powers of
#{-h(z,Az)}; on every smaller neighborhood these are bounded
by a polynomial in #{\|z\|} times #{\ue^{-\frac{\alpha}{2}\|z\|^2}}.
This integrable bound justifies differentiation under the
integral. The function #{\pi^n/\det K(w)} is holomorphic there
too. For #{w=it} with real #{|t|<\epsilon}, the operator
#{H+itA} is Hermitian positive, so the preceding calculation
identifies the two functions. These points accumulate at the
interior point zero. The one-variable identity theorem
therefore identifies the functions throughout the strip, in
particular at #{w=1}. The Grassmann determinant then proves
cancellation.}

\p{For the orthogonal splitting, #{u\ne0} gives
#{h(i_Eu,Ki_Eu)=h(u,Du)}, because the cross term lands in
#{R} and the sum is orthogonal. Thus #{D} is strictly
accretive, hence invertible. For the graph vector above,
#{p_EKx=0} and #{p_RKx=Sv}, so #{Kx=i_RSv}. Orthogonality
removes the eliminated component of #{x} from the product, and
#{h(x,Kx)=h(v,Sv)}. Therefore #{S} is strictly accretive.}
}

\p{For Hermitian positive #{K}, the normalized density
#{(\det K/\pi^n)\ue^{-h(z,Kz)}} is a genuine Gaussian probability
law on the underlying real space of dimension #{2n}. For a
general strictly accretive endomorphism the weight can be
complex, so there need not be a positive Gaussian law with
this density. \ref{fdip-00MH} records the value #{1+i}. A
single real Gaussian has only a determinant power #{-1/2}.
\vocabk{Grassmann algebra}{fdip-008F} by itself supplies no
convergence for a commuting integral.}

\p{\citet{Section 9.1, equations (9.3)-(9.5), p. 19}{wingate2025lattice}
describes integrating a fermion matrix and re-expressing its
determinant with commuting complex pseudofermions. Its equation
(9.5) uses #{K^{-1}} in the commuting quadratic form, yielding
a determinant rather than its inverse when convergence holds.
The cancellation here uses #{K} in both forms and the specified
factor #{\pi^{-n}}; the proof above states its convergence
hypotheses explicitly.}

\transclude{fdip-00MH}
